#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd5beb10-d637-54e1-a007-9706a5d51ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328392} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb54c0d-8e6b-5b2a-8326-ab4dd73e2d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328393} {:answer/id #uuid "3140949d-6243-520c-87d1-823e53d29c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328394} {:answer/id #uuid "da8bd8b7-b9b7-5c61-858f-19889bfb03d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328395} {:answer/id #uuid "12128697-5afc-509d-b095-b10903bdc8a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3140949d-6243-520c-87d1-823e53d29c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328394} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa056aa-e417-556d-9c3c-e03820513602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3fb283e6-c950-59d4-b793-9a73d1c9314e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115278" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals has an entire integrand that is odd about zero and opposite integration limits, so the standard odd-integrand cancellation rule applies directly?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "The criterion in this question is the direct odd-integrand cancellation rule: if the entire integrand is odd about zero and is integrable on $[-a,a]$, then its integral is zero.\n\nI satisfies that criterion. Its integrand $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is odd and continuous on $[-1,1]$, so $\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=0$.\n\nII does not satisfy the criterion because $\\sin x+\\cos x$ is not odd about zero: its sine term is odd but its cosine term is even. Nevertheless, the integral in II also has value zero. Both sine and cosine integrate to zero over the full period $[-\\pi,\\pi]$:\n\n$$\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}(\\sin x+\\cos x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[-\\cos x+\\sin x]_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}=1-1=0.$$\n\nFailing the odd-integrand criterion does not imply a nonzero integral; II's cancellation comes from its full-period structure.\n\nIII has an odd integrand, but the endpoints $-2$ and $4$ are not opposites. It therefore does not satisfy the direct criterion. Its value is\n\n$$\\int_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7x^3}{1+2x^4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac78[\\ln(1+2x^4)]_{-2}^{4}=\\frac78\\ln\\frac{513}{33}\\ne0.$$\n\nThus “I only” is correct for the stated direct odd-integrand criterion. If the question asked which integrals have value zero by any method, the answer would instead be I and II."}]}
